Florida lawmakers are scrambling to pass  new gun legislation for the first time  in more than a decade after the Parkland  school shooting pro-gun advocates  protested yesterday outside Florida's  Capitol the state Senate is expected to  vote today on a bill that would allow  teachers to be armed and restrict some  gun purchases Nicky batiste is at the  State Capitol in Tallahassee Nicky good  morning good morning bianna proposals to  ban assault style rifles and large  magazines were rejected over the weekend  though it briefly appeared that a pause  on ar-15 sales might pass the  republican-controlled chamber is  bitterly divided but must reach a  compromise by Friday to pass any new  laws speaking at this pro-gun rally  defiant college student 20-year old  Tiffany Berkley fought back against a  potential Florida law that would force  her to wait until she is 21 to buy a gun  raising the age would effectively  terminate any second Amendment rights I  have in this scene during a special  session Florida Senate spent nearly  eight hours amending the Marjory  Stoneman Douglas high school public  safety act the only thing that will help  the situation is if we ban assault  weapons it's not the weapon it's the  evil from within as it stands the  legislation would increase the age to  buy a rifle from 18 to 21 band bum  stocks and require a three day waiting  period for most gun purchases the  legislation will also start a new  marshal program permitting teachers and  staff to be armed after at least 132  hours of training the amendment is  adopted following a voice vote a  two-year ban on ar-15 assault weapons  initially passed but the motion was  reconsidered and reversed 20 minutes  later in a roll call vote  17 days 21 days mr. president the  decision stung for some Florida school  shooting survivors like Jackie Koren who  tweeted this breaks my heart but we will  not let this ruin our movement there  have been students from Stoneman Douglas  here fighting for gun control  what would you say to them you have a  right to be angry I'm angry with you but  we can't have our rights infringed  Brookley says she does support giving  teachers the right to be armed if the  Senate passes the bill today it will  then be considered by the house Gayle  all right Thank You Nicki
 

